round2.revised_rule:
List only data-dependent steps the report describes. A step is data-dependent if its result is estimated from the analysed data. These are:
- imputation;
- scaling;
- learned features (PCA or factor analysis, k-means groups, data-based cut-points, IQR outlier limits);
- data-driven predictor selection (including filtering by missingness, p-value or importance ranking);
- matching;
- resampling or class weighting;
- hyperparameter tuning;
- a stated choice of model, number of predictors or threshold made from results;
- recalibration.

Do not list:
- fixed-rule recoding or categorisation;
- formula-derived variables;
- exclusion of participants by fixed criteria;
- fixed settings;
- fitting of the model itself (including tree growing, splitting or pruning).

Printing several models' results, bold maxima or naming the best model is reporting, not selection.

Also record a step as 'not done (stated)' or 'not done (established: ...)' when the report states this or cited statements establish it (e.g. exclusion of participants with missing data -> no imputation). Fixed settings or silence establish neither tuning nor its absence.

Assign a scope only when cited statements together name the step and the data or partition it used. Narrative order, sample or root-node totals, generic cross-validation statements and unlinked figure labels do not fix a scope. Otherwise the scope is not_stated; quote any 'all data' phrase in the rationale.

Use test_outcomes_used only when the report states that outcomes of a validation or test set informed the choice and performance on that same set is reported.

Status:
- reported: at least one step has a scope other than not_stated, or a nonperformance is stated or established;
- unclear: data-dependent steps are described but every scope is not_stated;
- not_reported: neither a data-dependent step nor a nonperformance is described.

Never use not_applicable for this item.

